*.sh text eol=lf

# Certification evidence must round-trip byte-identically: the validator verifies each
# artifact's recorded SHA-256, and an LF->CRLF conversion on checkout would break every
# hash on a fresh clone. core.autocrlf is true on the certification workstation.
docs/production-certification/evidence/** -text
docs/production-certification/evidence/raw/** -text

# `agent check` compares every generated fact file byte for byte against a freshly generated
# one, and the generator writes LF. With core.autocrlf true — as it is on the Windows machines
# this project is developed on — checkout rewrites them to CRLF, so the comparison fails on a
# clean tree and the gate reports "1 stale" for a reason that has nothing to do with the facts
# being out of date. `npm run agent -- facts` then appears to fix it by writing LF back, which
# leaves the tree dirty with a zero-line diff and teaches people to ignore the check.
#
# Same reasoning as the certification evidence above: a file that is byte-compared must not be
# line-ending converted on the way in or out.
.agent/generated/** -text
